{"type":"thought","data":"The 6.6 literal in the"}
{"type":"thought","data":" plan is stale."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe plan claims to"}
{"type":"thought","data":" read a literal from"}
{"type":"thought","data":" the live SKILL that"}
{"type":"thought","data":" does not exist there."}
{"type":"thought","data":" The existing docs test"}
{"type":"thought","data":" already uses the correct"}
{"type":"thought","data":" fresh stem, so the "}
{"type":"thought","data":"implementation was fixed. However, the plan itself is still wrong. Design D5 also omits `_run$RUN`, which would regress the fresh-stem rule if pasted over the section."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe design body also"}
{"type":"thought","data":" contains the stale "}
{"type":"thought","data":"path. D5 uses `--timeout"}
{"type":"thought","data":" 1800` on `hmad-dispatch"}
{"type":"thought","data":" exec`, which is allowed"}
{"type":"thought","data":" because the dispatch verb itself accepts that flag; it does not violate the forbidden"}
{"type":"thought","data":" `timeout` binary pattern."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nD5 prescribes `rm "}
{"type":"thought","data":"-f \"$RP\"` on a stable path. This is the exact"}
{"type":"thought","data":" bug the fresh-stem "}
{"type":"thought","data":"rule was meant to prevent."}
{"type":"thought","data":" The live section avoids"}
{"type":"thought","data":" this by minting RUN,"}
{"type":"thought","data":" but the plan and design"}
{"type":"thought","data":" still include it."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThis is a build-class"}
{"type":"thought","data":" must-fix. The docs-half fixture and codex-leg recipe cite the stale path, so AC-3.5a is unsatisfiable against the tree. Re-applying D5 would also strip the run stamp."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe plan should be"}
{"type":"thought","data":" updated to match the"}
{"type":"thought","data":" tree rather than the"}
{"type":"thought","data":" tree drifting backward. This is an audit finding on the plan itself.\n\nThe plan incorrectly claims a test will fail, but the named test does not. I verified this by reading both the test and the CLI control flow. I did not run the mutation. The test discrimination invariant requires a controlled pair."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nI am confident the"}
{"type":"thought","data":" named test passes because"}
{"type":"thought","data":" `mkdir` on a file parent"}
{"type":"thought","data":" raises `NotADirectoryError`,"}
{"type":"thought","data":" which is caught as an `OSError` and produces"}
{"type":"thought","data":" the same exit marker."}
{"type":"thought","data":" The absent-root test"}
{"type":"thought","data":" is the one that would"}
{"type":"thought","data":" fail."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nI will not run the"}
{"type":"thought","data":" mutation harness because"}
{"type":"thought","data":" it edits production"}
{"type":"thought","data":" files even if it restores them. I can verify "}
{"type":"thought","data":"the pathlib behavior"}
{"type":"thought","data":" with a throwaway Python"}
{"type":"thought","data":" snippet in /tmp instead."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe test cannot distinguish the two failure modes. Both raise OperationalError before reaching mkdir, so they produce identical exit 2 markers and no COLLECT or traceback. This is a vacuous criterion under the test discrimination invariant. It is a measured analogue of AC-2.5b and must be fixed."}
